  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Easterly gap winds early this morning, then strengthening late\n   tonight into Sunday morning. Anticipate choppy lakes and \n   strong canyon winds.\n\n-  Widespread rain and mountain snow above 7,000 feet late tonight\n   into early Monday, mainly through central Idaho and northwest\n   Montana.\n\n-  Drying and warming conditions by mid-week.\n\nBrisk easterly winds across western Montana will persist through \nSunday morning, with gusts up to 30 mph expected near favored \ncanyons, along the western slopes of major terrain, and over \nregional waters like Flathead Lake. The strongest winds will be \nmost pronounced this evening into early Sunday morning. Otherwise,\nconditions across western Montana and north central Idaho will \nremain largely dry and slightly warmer today ahead of an \napproaching storm system.\n\nA strong low pressure system will sweep across the region late \ntonight through Sunday, bringing widespread showers and isolated \nthunderstorms. The storm's northern track favors the heaviest \nprecipitation across north-central Idaho and northwest Montana, \nwhere half an inch to over an inch of rain is forecast. Snow \nlevels will fall to around 7,000 feet by Sunday night in these \nareas. Backcountry recreationists should prepare for cold, wet, \nand raw conditions that will significantly increase the risk of \nhypothermia for anyone caught unprepared. Further south and east, \nincluding the Missoula area, precipitation amounts will be notably\nlighter. Southwest Montana will remain mostly dry with gusty \nwesterly winds, resulting in near-critical fire weather \nconditions.\n\nThe system will depart to the east by Tuesday, transitioning the \nregion to a generally warmer and drier weather pattern through \nmid-week. Despite the overall drying trend, a few weak atmospheric\ndisturbances may still trigger isolated, light showers. Tuesday \nmorning has the potential to be quite chilly, particularly in the \nhigher valleys of southwest Montana and along the Continental \nDivide, where clearing overnight skies may allow temperatures to \ndrop sharply.",
